WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.720
Yakitate!! 9!

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:01.980
The fifth round.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:02.940
Shinanomachi
The fifth round.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.700
The Pantasia team, consisting of Kazuma, Kanmuri and Kawachi,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.150
is now the winner
of consecutive matches.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.690
The Austrian Court Chef
Remarkable!! Tsutsumi Masanobu, the first Japanese to ever pass!

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:11.500
St. Pierre brought forth
Tsutsumi Masanobu,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.080
Austrian court chef and Kanmuri’s brother by blood, as the opponent.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.830
Very well, we will now begin the fifth match
of Yakitate!! 9 here in Shinanomachi.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.210
Begin!

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.300
He’s going to use that to make warm jam.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.640
Warm jam?

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.380
I’ll get this done in one go!

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:37.680
Mr. Judge.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.180
I request that you sample my item.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.560
This is very dangerous
Please do not imitate it at any cost!

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.020
He put his face into the flaming pot!

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:52.820
Th-This is...

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.320
I-I can see it!

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.910
The superhero of all children.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.580
The warrior of justice made of sweet bean jam!

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.040
D-Don’t tell me...

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.620
The person making the superhero is...

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.410
That’s right!

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.790
The real person making him is....

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.180
Uncle Jam!

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:16.680
He’s burning the apple tree wood
inside the drum canister to smoke the jam.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.640
It’s done.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.430
All right then.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.140
Monkey bars?!

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:26.730
Don’t tell me...

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.360
Monkey bars! Is that so?!

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.610
Is that it?!

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.660
You preserved the flavor of the rhubarb 100%
by using the freeze-concentration technique.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.540
In addition, your strategy of
smoking it using apple tree wood.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.370
This also enters the class of
ultimate among ultimate jams.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.920
Therefore this match is...

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.640
Devoid of winners.
I declare it a draw!

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.470
Kazuma was down with a high fever, but..

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:04.860
Kanmuri managed to bring the match
over his brother’s specialty, jam, to a draw.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:05.350
But...

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.190
For the first time, a brake was
placed on their succession of victories.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:14.190
Finally, an opponent that could stand up to
Kazuma and company’s skill had been found!

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.690
Kirisaki Yuuichi.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.990
Who can he possibly...

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.280
bring in as the next opponent...?

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.160
Good morning!

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.290
Azuma’s cold is cured.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.630
And Kanmuri’s successor problem has been solved.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.170
I’m glad that everything has been solved cleanly.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.420
Right?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.930
I’d felt from the very beginning that
Manager was more suited for that sort of job.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.180
Very true.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.100
What’s wrong, Bro?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.440
You don’t look well.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.940
Are you all right, Bro?

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.770
Cheer up, Bro.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.610
Shut up!

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:54.860
Don’t call me Bro!

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.650
And stop following me!

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.570
Please don’t say that, Bro!

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.660
Looks like he’s still resisting, but...

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.500
I’m sure that Manager will realize that he should
change occupations to a thug soon enough.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.580
The fifth match was truly a big one,
with a series of good things happening.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.960
No, that’s not quite true.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.670
What is it, Kanmuri?

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.420
Do we still have a problem?

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.420
Yeah, actually...

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.270
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.100
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.440
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.900
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.990
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.490
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:53.550
I want this voice to reach you, even though it’s still a tiny song.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:59.720
That’s because I have the feeling that something’s gonna start.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:14.860
As I was cowering, muffling my voice, the tears that I shed

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:23.410
probably helped me notice that

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:29.330
I’ve almost forgotten how to believe.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:38.180
The future that I painted (I will fulfill it)

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:43.550
Looking up to the clear skies,

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:50.690
I begin to run once again. Someday, I want to dye

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:56.480
that white sky with my colors.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:03.530
I’m going to fly as far as possible, shining like a marble,

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.870
unfolding these wings of mine.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.260
Seaweed bread showdown!!

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.600
A really famous person is gonna appear, you know!

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.300
What was that?!

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:26.600
When it’s a draw, the next person to win
automatically gets two panels?!

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.510
That’s right.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.260
Th... Then, should we lose the next match...

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.560
Two panels will be taken away?

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.060
No, that’s not all.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.270
Right now, the panels look like this.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:41.110
Should the opponent choose panel #7,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:46.450
since there’s a panel in between 7 and 9,
three panels will be stolen in one go.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.410
Also, since this match is going to be really important,

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:54.540
I’m sure that the St. Pierre side will
bring out a really, really strong opponent.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.500
There is no doubt that the
next match will be a hard one.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.550
H-How can this be...?

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:10.050
It could be that this is what we truly have to fear
from the panel battle system of Yakitate!! 9.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:14.050
After all, it is Kirisaki who planned out this battle.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.680
Hey, guys!

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.480
It’s not like we’ve lost yet,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.860
so why’re you so blue?

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.560
All we have to do is make our bread as always,
without worrying about our opponent.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.780
Th-That’s right!

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.610
Yeah, you’re right.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.910
There’s no point in getting depressed over an
opponent we don’t know anything about yet.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.710
Let’s head to the match with confidence.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.540
Yeah!

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.220
Well, I did say that, but...

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.970
If I said that I’m not worried about our
opponent for this round, it would be a lie.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:58.770
Until now, St. Pierre has not won a single match.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.980
It would be strange for Kirisaki
not to play his trump card here.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.360
H-Hey, Kanmuri...

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.860
Who’s that man?

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:06.690
Hmm?

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.990
It... It can’t be that that guy
is our next opponent, right?

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.450
N-No way!

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:14.700
I don’t believe it!

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.700
Hey, you guys!

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.200
Didn’t I say this already?

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.950
We have to stop worrying about our opponent.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.960
It makes no difference who our opponent might be.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.130
Yeah, but well...

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.840
I think that even you would be surprised at that.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.760
I-Isn’t that guy from Momoya?

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:37.350
Yeah.
Note: Momoya (<b>桃屋) is a brand name for all kinds of sauces and condiments to be eaten along with food.</b>

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.020
That person’s name is Miki Norihei.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.400
In 1996, he was awarded the Order of
the Rising Sun, 4th Class, Small Ribbon.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.850
He’s one of Japan’s heroes.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:47.820
I-I can’t believe it.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.360
To think that they brought in someone this big...

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.740
I wonder if they’re all right with all the rights issues...

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:54.820
I-I wouldn’t know...

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:59.080
It may be that they’ve come to this battle resolved
that the broadcast may be interrupted at any time.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.750
Th... That’s some awesome resolve.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:02.830
What’s that supposed to mean?

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.580
Either way...

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.800
We now have no choice but to pray.

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:09.550
F... For what?

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.300
That the theme city’s specialty product is not <i>that</i>.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.430
“That”?

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.050
You mean that?!

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:21.270
Very well then, we will now have the
choosing of panels for the sixth match.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.390
St. Pierre gets choice of panels.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:30.860
Seven.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.070
Uppurui

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.320
I-It’s over!

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.030
Juurokujima?

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:36.410
Uppurui

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:39.790
No. That’s read as Uppurui.
Uppurui

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:42.040
The world’s highest-quality seaweed, Uppurui seaweed.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:44.800
This is the Uppurui
famous for producing it.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.800
Looks like it is Uppurui.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:49.800
Uppurui
Uppurui refers to Uppurui in the
city of Izumo of Shimane prefecture.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.800
Uppurui refers to Uppurui in the
city of Izumo of Shimane prefecture.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:53.340
<b>Daimyo
Matsudaira Fumai</b>

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.800
In order to spread the word to everyone
about just how delicious Uppurui seaweed was,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:57.300
<b>Edo just ahead</b>

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.850
the daimyo who ruled over this city, Matsudaira Fumai,

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:03.310
once went to the city of Edo
wearing an overcoat made of seaweed.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:04.810
It is said that in the city,

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.820
he stunned the folks there by breaking
pieces off and eating it, over and over again.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.070
We expect that you use various strategies
to make a bread that would stun people.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:15.080
That’s all.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:17.160
Uppurui

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.660
Oh, I almost forgot.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:19.990
How careless of me.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:22.540
Looks like I’d forgotten about “Yakitate Chance.”

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.040
Yakitate Chance?

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.160
Yeah.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:25.170
Uppurui

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.170
Panels which bear the mark of this star
are called Yakitate Chance panels.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:33.920
In addition to the panel that was a draw
and panel #7 chosen this time,

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.130
you’re allowed to pick one more panel of your choice.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.640
Any panel other than 7 or 9 is
you’re allowed to pick one more panel of your choice.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:38.930
In other words...

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:42.510
If you win this match, in addition to
the panel from the previous match’s draw,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.850
you can gain up to three panels in one go.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:45.640
What was that?!

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.110
W-We’ve never heard of this!

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:49.150
Shut up!

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.730
That’s the rules, so it can’t be helped.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:52.940
If you don’t like it, then just give up.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:54.080
That’s all. Hmph.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.080
No way...

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.240
This is the pits.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.080
To think that we have to battle it out
with seaweed against such an awesome guy.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.160
Yeah.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.750
To top it off, should we lose, the
enemy will probably choose panel #1.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:16.590
So, just by winning in this one match,
the enemy will be able to grab five panels.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.050
Don’t these rules remind you of the
old quiz shows where they’d cheat a lot?

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.100
You said it.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.600
Azuma Kazuma-kun, I presume...

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:28.480
Oh, you are...

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.900
I’m honored to meet you.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.240
I’m Miki Norihei.
Coincidentally, there’s an additional pun in Nonhei’s name: Nori means seaweed.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:33.730
Coincidentally, there’s an additional pun in Nonhei’s name: Nori means seaweed.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:34.940
Pleased to meet you too.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.420
Actually, I became very interested

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:42.200
when I heard that you were aiming for a Japanese bread, the <i>Japan</i>.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:47.910
So, I requested the owner of St. Pierre to
permit me to battle against you, Azuma-kun.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.410
I-Is that so...?

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.670
Well, instead of small talk,
would you try eating this?

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:58.550
This is Gohan Desuyo, which
I’ve changed to better suit bread.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.560
You just spread it on toast like this.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:03.180
Come, try it?

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.890
Th-Thank you.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.600
Well, here I go.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.480
Th-This is...!

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:15.860
How is it?

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.520
Isn’t it quite good?

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.500
I plan on improving it even further for the actual match,

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.820
and plan to use it as a filling for bread.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.700
Let’s both fight it out fair and square, okay?

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.330
H-How was it, Azuma-kun?

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.170
Was it really amazing?

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:35.500
Yeah.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:39.420
There’s no doubt that he’s stronger than the
strongest of all the opponents we’ve faced so far.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.090
But... But still, you didn’t even have a reaction.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.640
So it’s not like we’ve lost all hope of winning, right?

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.890
But isn’t that just because it was Azuma-kun eating it?

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.400
After all, up until now, we’ve not
really seen him having many reactions...

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:58.940
Th... That’s true, I guess...

00:11:58.940 --> 00:11:59.860
No.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.280
He stole a reaction from me!

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:03.700
What was that?!

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:10.170
Rewinding

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:12.660
This is...!

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:14.670
The program’s title has changed!

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:18.130
The logo’s been changed to “It’s Yakitate!! Japan!”

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:20.050
I didn’t notice!

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.460
That’s not all!

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:26.220
The newspapers and TV programs for the past
few days have become “It’s Yakitate!! Japan!”

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:27.800
What was that?!

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.890
And...

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.220
There’s more?

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.640
Both volume 18 of the graphic novel
and the preview commercials...

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:38.480
In addition to the “Yakitate!! Japan,”
there’s an “It’s” added to all of them!

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.610
I-It can’t be!

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:42.150
I-It can’t get any worse!

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:46.240
A reaction that affects each
and every title is unheard of!

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.280
Yeah.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.780
This is truly the most amazing reaction ever!

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:53.120
It’s causing an unbelievable
amount of trouble to everyone!

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:55.340
But, I’m sure that this is...

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.090
the true strength of that man.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:13.470
Oh!

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.680
The scenery in this place is really pretty.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:16.690
Yeah, really.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:20.520
Yeah, but whether or not the scenery is pretty,

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:24.440
the specialty product here in Uppurui is...
seaweed, seaweed and more seaweed.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:28.360
Yeah, this time we really
can’t do anything with that angle.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:31.580
Our opponent in a match
using seaweed is Miki Norihei...

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.910
There’s no way that we can win against him.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.040
Yeah, we’ve tried so hard to get this far,

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:38.580
and now, losing five panels
leaves you really feeling hopeless.

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.340
No, that’s not true.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.420
What we’re doing in this round is a bread battle.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:47.050
Even if we lose at making the seaweed,

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:49.350
if our bread is good, we have plenty of hope of winning.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:51.680
Well, that’s true...

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.600
Th-That’s right!

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:57.890
Unlike the jam battle we had last time,
this time we can make the bread itself.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:00.270
If we fight back using bread,
we might be able to win!

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:01.230
Right?

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:02.310
All right!

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:03.360
Since we’ve decided on that,

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:07.030
let’s get us some seaweed right away
and concentrate on making the bread!

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.360
Yeah!

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.330
This is Uppurui seaweed, huh?

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:16.160
Yeah.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.830
If you boil the nori you get here in
a pot and scoop it with chopsticks,

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.170
it shines like ink on the tip of a brush.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:23.710
So, it’s called Fude-nori.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:27.550
A hundred grams of it can get priced anywhere
from thousands of yen up to 20,000 yen.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:31.090
The glossy luster of this seaweed is
incomparable to that of the regular stuff.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.600
A... A hundred grams for 20,000?!

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.600
Well yeah, that’s some pretty special seaweed...

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:37.810
But, Kanmuri...

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.600
It’s true that this seaweed looks really delicious,

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.560
but would it be good preserved with soy?

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:44.400
If we’re to make it go well with bread,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.820
then I think that it’s probably best to preserve it with soy, but...

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.200
This seaweed is strong enough to be made into an overcoat, right?

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.820
So, what should we do to make it tender?

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.530
That’s what the trouble is...

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:59.790
What makes seaweed preserved in soy delicious is
that tender consistency that melts in your mouth.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.500
Since <i>he</i> is our opponent,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.790
we also have to come up with a way to make our seaweed as tender as possible.

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:09.300
So that means that even if we’re to fight back
using bread, we’ve no hope of winning, huh?

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:10.550
Yeah.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:11.630
It’s all right!

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:13.430
I’ve thought of a way to do that!

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.680
We’re going to use this!

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:19.180
Th... That is...!

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.390
Isn’t that just regular Pepmi-Cola?

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:22.230
That’s right.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:27.780
Well, I do think that if you’re going
to drink cola, it has to be Pepmi, but...

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.990
What does that have to do with seaweed, anyway?

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.780
We’re going to boil it.

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.740
We’re going to boil it in this Pepmi-Cola!

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:35.910
I see!

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:37.000
Brewing it in cola, huh?

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.080
Wh-What’s that supposed to be?

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:40.120
Just as the name implies...

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.870
It’s a strategy involving brewing something in cola.

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:46.000
It’s a technique used occasionally in
really high-class restaurants as well.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.960
Haven’t you heard of it?

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:49.510
N-Not really...

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:53.300
It refers to a technique where things
like various meats or marine plants like kelp

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.760
can be softened by brewing them in cola
because of the carbon dioxide present in it.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.350
Seaweed is a marine plant like kelp, after all...

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.970
So, it should work well with seaweed as well.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:05.060
Well that’s good, but how does it taste?

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:09.980
Even if the food item does becomes soft,
it’s useless if it doesn’t taste good.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.650
All right then,
I’ll make some for you to try.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:14.660
H-Hey, Azuma!

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.030
Hey Mister, lend me a pot?

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:17.780
A pot?

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.280
And if possible, a cooking stove.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:21.370
Also, if you have chopsticks and a plate, it would be great.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.580
Is this for real?

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.460
We’ve had some pretty strange
strategies up until now, but...

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.090
I don’t think you’d find much stranger than this.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.640
Look at that.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.850
We’re being laughed at by the local girls.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.400
This may very well be used at
high-class restaurants, but...

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:50.900
it’s not possible to make flavored seaweed that easily.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:51.940
I guess this is it.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:55.950
Come, try some.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.700
E... Even if you want me to try some...

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:01.410
I really don’t want to try it.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.750
Eat it quickly.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:03.160
F-Fine already!
Eat it quickly.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:04.660
F-Fine already!

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.630
Why do I have to do this?!

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.630
Th-This is...!

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.340
Oh no, Azuma-kun!

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.310
That little girl from before is drowning in the sea!

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:17.340
What was that?!

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.600
Help me!

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:23.180
We must swim to help her immediately!

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:23.980
Yeah.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.310
Wait, you guys!

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.480
The currents are very strong in that area.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:29.560
If an amateur goes in there, he’s sure to drown!

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.320
N-No way...

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:36.200
That’s...!

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:45.120
Help me!

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:51.710
It’s Kawachi-san!

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:53.380
No, it’s Pepmiman!

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.470
Thank you very much, Pepmiman.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:05.890
I won’t play by myself near the sea anymore.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:09.940
Sorry about that.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:12.820
It looks like he can’t say
anything but “shuwaa” right now.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:14.190
Is that so?

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:16.900
Well then, tell him thank you for me later.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:18.530
Sure, no problem.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:20.610
Bye bye.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:24.330
While it may have been because you
were in the middle of your cola reaction,

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:27.000
you did something truly wonderful, Kawachi-san!

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:31.420
He’s kinda annoying like this.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:34.710
Well then, how about we head to the lodge?

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:35.300
Sure.

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:39.930
Note: Kuro means “black” when written as <b>黒.
So, “looks like it will be black.”</b>

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.340
Geez.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:44.430
It’s impressive how you can find
these weird places to stay every time.

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:49.940
While this may be Japan’s #1 place for seaweed...

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.520
is it really necessary to make
everything pitch black like this?

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.520
I don’t believe it.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:56.900
Even the room’s hanging scrolls are pitch black.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:00.110
Couldn’t you just have remained Pepmiman for life?

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:01.860
Huh? Did you say something?

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:02.780
No, not really.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.120
So what should we do, Azuma-kun?

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.080
About the bread, that is.

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:14.380
No matter what we do with the seaweed,
I don’t think we can win against Norihei-san.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:16.590
So, I think this will be more than enough.

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:19.090
This time there are five panels on the line.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:22.050
Since we can’t win with the seaweed,
we must overwhelm them with the bread.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.470
Don’t worry.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.260
I’ve thought of something for that already.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:26.230
Huh?

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:30.230
All we need to do is make the
dough fluffy like freshly made rice.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.600
Oh, what a wonderful idea!

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:37.440
Since the filling is seaweed, all we need to do
is make the dough fluffy like freshly made rice!

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.440
What are you, an idiot?!

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:40.690
Listen up, Azuma.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:46.450
To make a fluffy bread like freshly made rice
is considered to be a dream for all bakers.

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:50.750
Even if you’re a genius, something
like that can’t possibly be easy...

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:51.540
It’s possible.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:53.460
Right? It can’t possibly be done...

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:55.330
What was that?!

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.090
Something like that is easy.

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.840
A... Are you for real?

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:01.760
Say, more importantly...

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:04.510
Have you ever made spring rolls, Kanmuri?

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.600
No, I haven’t, but...

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:08.560
I know how to make them.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:09.890
Is that so?

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.310
In that case, we should be okay.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.390
S-Spring rolls?

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.480
Hey, what do you mean?

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:21.280
What do spring rolls have to do with
bread that’s fluffy like freshly made rice?

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.280
Nothing whatsoever.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:25.820
What’s that supposed to mean?!

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.950
Relax, relax.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.830
Look forward to finding out the rest during the match.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.090
What’s this?

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:43.100
The crowd is much more
enthusiastic about this than usual.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:46.090
They even have spotlights set up this time.

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:49.100
Is some famous actor or
someone coming to the match?

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:51.600
I-It’s crazy Kawachi-san!

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:55.980
Huh? What’s wrong?

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:57.600
Th-The audience is...

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:00.190
Yeah, they’ve been really loud for a while.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:03.620
Let me guess, they’ve called in
someone famous to raise the ratings?

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:04.990
No!

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:09.620
It’s because Norihei-san was giving out
seaweed preserved in soy for free earlier.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:10.700
Here you go!

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:12.620
It’s Gohan Desuyo!

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:13.750
Huh?

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:22.760
Norihei!

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.130
It’s Norihei?!
Norihei!

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:37.900
Norihei!

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:40.270
E... Everyone’s really into this.

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:41.400
That’s right.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.360
Due to their reaction to the seaweed,
they’re all really getting into this.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.530
Yay!

00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:57.750
Very well then.

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:01.050
We will now start the sixth match
of Yakitate!! 9 here in Uppurui.

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:02.250
Begin!

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.170
Looks like the match finally begins.

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:07.090
Yeah.

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:11.470
In addition to changing the title, the enemy has
brought the audience over to his side as well.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:14.970
We really might have to call him
the most amazing man on earth.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:18.440
But Azuma, we can’t lose this match.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.940
This most important match has five panels at stake.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:26.200
Even if all the audience
doesn’t believe in it, I... I alone...

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:28.950
believe that you will win!

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:44.010
Robbie, come on out to the sound of the voice calling you.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:51.930
Robbie, you were crying all by yourself.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:23:00.310
Robbie, the eyes in your heart...

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:03.780
what is it that they reflect?

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:07.200
...Is it “Who am I?”...

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:15.000
The other side of the mirage flickers faintly.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:23.550
Anywhere is fine, as long as it’s not here.

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:32.300
My dancing heart is reborn as a marble time and again.

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:36.390
The colors I just filled in were yours.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:40.310
It’s not something that will finish with a turn. Be load.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:48.280
Just like your joy, try letting your tears flow.

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:52.330
See? The world is filled in with your colors.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:59.000
Like a tumble, do the best of you.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.180
Even if the whole world turns against us...

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.100
I will believe in you, Azuma!

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:11.480
Well then, I’m going to get serious as well.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.140
If you do that, we can’t win!

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:14.590
You really are a failure of a human being.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:16.230
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.230
A traditional taste!!

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:18.860
The heart that thinks of Japan is just one!

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:19.890
Be sure to watch.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.650
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:25.240
The reason seaweed is delicious is that like
dry bonito, konbu and shiitake mushrooms,

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.120
it contains glutamic acid,
inosinic acid and guanylic acid.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:28.490
The main components that give umami to Japanese food
it contains glutamic acid,
inosinic acid and guanylic acid.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.660
These form the main components
that give umami to Japanese food.
The main components that give umami to Japanese food

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:34.250
Oh?
